cff-version: 1.2.0
message: Please cite the following works when using this project.
abstract: >-
  Heterogeneous-agent models with multiple decisions face a computational
  dilemma. Joint optimization over all choices is slow; strong separability
  restrictions speed computation but rule out economically interesting
  interactions. I introduce Sequential EGM (EGM^n), which eases this tension by
  decomposing the problem into sequential stages so that each stage can exploit
  the Endogenous Grid Method’s closed-form inversion, chaining speed gains
  across decisions wherever separable utility or an invertible transition
  permits it. The resulting curvilinear grids require interpolation that
  respects their structure, so I develop ENGINE (ENdogenous Grid INterpolation
  and Extrapolation), which exploits the index structure inherited from
  exogenous grids, with no construction step and no triangulation. In the
  canonical liquid-illiquid household block, EGM^n solves an order of magnitude
  faster than the nested method in serial and 3.7 to 7.9 times faster at sixteen
  threads. Replacing existing curvilinear interpolation with ENGINE cuts
  full-solve time 2.8- to 4.5-fold at matched accuracy.
title: The Sequential Endogenous Grid Method
